## Phase 1: Gather lightweight intent

A bootstrap flow does NOT need the full `/design-consultation` product context. We only need enough to narrow the catalog from 156 entries to ~5. Single AskUserQuestion:

> "I'll install a DESIGN.md from the designdotmd.directory catalog (156 spec-compliant designs). Which **feel** fits your product? Pick one or describe in your own words:"
>
> - A) **Technical / dark / monospace** — developer tools, CLIs, dev dashboards (peers: terminal, obsidian, zed-dev)
> - B) **Editorial / serif / warm** — content-heavy sites, magazines, reading experiences (peers: heritage, broadsheet-01, wine-country)
> - C) **Minimal / sans / corporate** — SaaS products, B2B, shipping-ready (peers: swiss-grid, vercel-ink, cobalt-product)
> - D) **Playful / bold / warm** — consumer apps, kids, games (peers: candy-shop, pastel-candy, arcade-neon-pop)
> - E) **Finance / trading / data-dense** (peers: defi-chrome, exchange-tick, neobank-mint)
> - F) **Other — describe your vibe in a sentence**

If the user picks F or describes, map to the closest catalog category via the `category` command:

```bash
npx --yes designdotmd category 2>&1 | head -50
```

---

## Phase 2: Shortlist from the catalog

Fetch the entries for the chosen category:

```bash
npx --yes designdotmd category "<category-name>" 2>&1
```

If the user's intent spans multiple categories (e.g., "technical and playful"), pull both lists and deduplicate.

Present the top 3-5 as an AskUserQuestion with each option's tagline from the listing. The listing output includes tags and a shareable URL per entry — include both in the option labels so the user can preview on the web if they want.

Example option format:

> - A) **terminal** — *"Phosphor green on deep black. Fast, loud, honest."* (Mono, Dark, Technical)
> - B) **obsidian** — *"Volcanic glass, phosphor purple, edge."* (Dark, Mono, Technical)
> - C) **zed-dev** — *"Editor-dark, warmer than pitch, glyph-sharp."* (Technical, Dark, Mono)
> - D) Show me more options in this category
> - E) Go back and pick a different vibe

If the user wants to preview before committing, run `npx designdotmd add <id>` into a throwaway `/tmp` directory and `cat` the file to show the tokens + prose before asking them to commit.

---

## Phase 3: Install the chosen design

Run the CLI and normalize the filename (the catalog CLI writes lowercase `design.md`; gstack convention is uppercase `DESIGN.md`):

```bash
npx --yes designdotmd add <chosen-id>
if [ -f design.md ] && [ ! -f DESIGN.md ]; then
  mv design.md DESIGN.md
elif [ -f design.md ] && [ -f DESIGN.md ]; then
  # Should not happen given Phase 0, but guard anyway
  echo "ERROR: both design.md and DESIGN.md exist. Manual intervention needed."
  exit 1
fi
```

---

## Phase 4: Spec-lint verification

```bash
npx --yes @google/design.md lint DESIGN.md --format json > /tmp/design-bootstrap-lint-$$.json 2>&1
cat /tmp/design-bootstrap-lint-$$.json
```

Parse the output. Three scenarios:

- **`errors > 0`:** unexpected — all catalog entries should lint clean. Show the error and tell the user this is a catalog-quality issue, not a gstack issue. Suggest reporting to the directory maintainer (`@bidah` on X, or `rodrigofigueroa.name@gmail.com`). Offer to keep the file anyway or revert.
- **`errors == 0 && warnings > 0`:** normal for all catalog entries (typically 2 "unused secondary/neutral" warnings). Summarize one-line: *"Lint passed: 0 errors, N unused-token warnings (expected for template entries — tokens are defined but not yet referenced by components you'll add later)."*
- **`errors == 0 && warnings == 0`:** rare, announce cleanly.

Record lint result as part of the skill's output so downstream skills (`design-review`, `design-html`, `plan-design-review`) know the file is verified.

---

## Phase 5: Append decisions log and hand-off

Open the new DESIGN.md and append a Decisions Log entry (creating the section if it doesn't exist):

```markdown
## Decisions Log

*Non-standard section — preserved per spec rule on unknown sections.*

| Date | Decision | Rationale |
|:-----|:---------|:----------|
| <today> | Bootstrapped from designdotmd.directory catalog entry `<id>` | Fast path: started from a proven template rather than custom-building. Lint: 0 errors, N warnings. Source: https://designdotmd.directory/#/d/<id> |
```

This keeps provenance explicit. If the design is later customized, the log shows what it diverged from.

**Update CLAUDE.md** (or create if absent) — same block as `/design-consultation`:

```markdown
## Design System
Always read DESIGN.md before making any visual or UI decisions.
All font choices, colors, spacing, and aesthetic direction are defined there.
DESIGN.md follows the Google Labs DESIGN.md spec (alpha): YAML tokens are
normative; prose explains rationale.
```

**Hand-off guidance:**

Tell the user:

> Installed `<id>` as DESIGN.md. This is a **starting point**, not a finished system — the catalog entries have ~6 colors and 2-4 components, while a production design system typically needs 15-20 colors and 10+ components. Two natural next steps:
>
> - **Customize in place:** run `/design-consultation update` for a guided pass that expands tokens and adds components while preserving what you picked.
> - **Preview what it looks like:** run `/design-html` to render a working page using the tokens, so you can see the design before writing app code.
>
> The provenance is in the Decisions Log — you can always see what you started from.

